+## Findings
+
+| Id | Severity | Finding | Fix / decision | Test |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| SEC-1 | medium | **Zip bomb in document extraction.** `read_file` on a `.docx/.xlsx/.pptx/.odt/.ods/.odp/.epub` checked only the 50 MB input size; a part declaring tens of GB (deflate reaches ~1000:1) was read whole into memory by our XML readers and the third-party ones, enough to kill a long-running daemon serving every profile. Reachable from any document the model is asked to read (a repository, a download, a chat attachment). | **fixed**: `extract._check_zip_budget` refuses more than 10,000 parts or 256 MB declared before any reader opens the file (`zipfile` never yields more than a part's declared size, so this bounds every reader) | `tests/security/test_archives.py::test_doc_zip_bomb_is_refused_before_any_reader_runs` |
+| SEC-2 | low | **Webhook timestamp crash.** `verify_signature` accepted any `str.isdigit()` value in the unauthenticated `X-Indus-Timestamp` header and passed it to `int()`: `²`, `①` or a 5,000-digit value raised instead of answering 401 (a 500 with a traceback in the log for every such request). | **fixed**: ASCII digits only, at most 15 | `tests/security/test_trust_boundaries.py::test_hook_odd_timestamp_is_a_refusal_not_a_crash` |
+| SEC-3 | medium | **A2A answers were not fenced.** `a2a_call` returns text another organisation's agent wrote, but was not on the untrusted list, so it reached the model as plain tool output (indirect prompt injection), while inbound A2A text was already framed as untrusted. | **fixed**: `a2a_call` added to `core.untrusted.UNTRUSTED_NAMES` | `tests/security/test_trust_boundaries.py::test_inject_outside_text_reaches_the_model_fenced` |
+| SEC-4 | medium | **Fence laundering through scripts and subagents.** A script run by `execute_code` can call `web_extract` / `web_search` over RPC and print the page; the script's output comes back under `execute_code`, unfenced. `delegate_task` returns a subagent's summary of fenced content unfenced (lower risk: model-written). | **open** (needs a design choice, not a one-line fix: fencing RPC answers breaks scripts that parse JSON). Proposal: the code tool records that a run called an untrusted tool and the tool round fences that run's result. Owner: lead, P25 follow-up. | — |
+| SEC-5 | low | **`message_agent` answers from peer gateways are not fenced.** Replies from another machine's bot reach the model as plain text. Local profiles are the owner's own. | **open**, lead decision: fence answers from `peer/profile` targets (proposal) or accept. | — |
+| SEC-6 | info | **`str.isdigit()` then `int()` on outside text** appears in about 30 adapter and route spots (non-ASCII digits raise `ValueError`). Most are after authentication or on platform-validated fields (`Content-Length` is validated by the HTTP server first); none found pre-auth besides SEC-2. | **open**: follow-up sweep to `isascii() and isdigit()`; not a static rule (too noisy) | — |
+| SEC-7 | low | **Non-http `Origin` accepted on loopback binds** (`HostRules.accepts_origin`): needed by the desktop's own scheme; `Origin: null` from a sandboxed page passes the origin check. A credential is still required on every route and socket (B-WEB-2, B-WEB-4), and the loopback mode uses no cookies, so no request rides ambient authority. | **accepted residual** (documented); revisit if a cookie ever authenticates loopback | `tests/unit/web/auth/test_host_guard.py` |
+| SEC-8 | low | **Spawns that inherit the process env**: the dashboard's git update of a checkout install (`web/routes/system.py`), doctor/locate probes, the user's editor. The env can hold provider keys exported in the launching shell. | **allow-listed with reasons** (each runs the person's own tools); follow-up: give the dashboard's git calls a child env once their proxy/ssh needs are listed | `tooling/checks/tests/test_security_static.py` |
+| SEC-9 | info | **Webhook `auth: auto` accepts an untimestamped generic HMAC** (replayable after the one-hour delivery-id window). | already an owner decision (HANDOFF "webhook `auth: auto` replay"); unchanged | `tests/contract/gateway/test_webhook.py` |
+| SEC-10 | info | **Fence threshold**: outside text shorter than 32 characters is not fenced (`untrusted.MIN_CHARS`). Short text can still carry an instruction. | accepted residual (lead may lower it; golden prompts unaffected) | `tests/unit/core/test_tool_round.py` |
+| SEC-11 | medium | **PNG decompression bomb without Pillow.** Pillow is optional and absent by default; the fallback reader (`imaging._png_inflate`, used by `validate_decodable`, `fit`, `crop`) ran `zlib.decompress` on the whole IDAT stream. A 1x1 PNG of a few hundred KB passes the header limits and inflates to gigabytes. `vision_analyze` reaches it with any image URL the model is given, including from the webhook floor. | **fixed**: the stream inflates at most to the exact size the header declares (`_png_raw_size`, Adam7 included) | `tests/security/test_archives.py::test_media_png_stream_inflates_only_to_its_declared_size` |
+
+## Not covered here
+
+- Browser-side proofs for the dashboard and the desktop (CSP on every page, IPC from a foreign
+  frame, fuses on the packaged binary, guest isolation): P25-03.
+- Dependency vulnerabilities, licences and pin provenance: P25-04.
+- Release signing (P23-15/16) and the canary rehearsal (P25-22).
